              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 1.2: Structure of the coordinate plane"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.506,
            0.889,
            0.602
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Point B is on the x-axis and 4.5 units to the right of O, its coordinates are (4.5, 0). This fact is written as B = (4.5, 0). Point G is on the y-axis and 4.5 units downward from O, its coordinates are (0, – 4.5), that is, G = (0, – 4.5). Point H is also on the y-axis but 4 units above O, its coordinates are (0, 4), that is, H = (0, 4)."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.602,
            0.889,
            0.66
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "A point P = (x, 0) lies on the x-axis. If x is positive, then P lies to the right of O. If x is negative, P lies to the left of O. A point P = (0, y) lies on the y-axis. If y is positive, P lies above O; if y is negative, P lies below O."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.66,
            0.889,
            0.719
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While writing the coordinates of a point, it is often convenient to drop the ‘=’ sign and write P = (x, y) simply as P (x, y). This is especially true while marking points on a graph."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 7,
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.748,
            0.626,
            0.776
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Exercise Set 1.1"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.109,
            0.795,
            0.889,
            0.836
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Fig. 1.3 shows Reiaan’s room with points OABC marking its corners. The x- and y-axes are marked in the figure. Point O is the origin."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
